EXPERIMENT 27 – VOTING ELIGIBILITY USING BOUNDARY VALUE ANALYSIS (JUNIT)
AIM
To write JUnit test cases to validate voting eligibility based on age using Boundary Value Analysis in JUnit.
ALGORITHM
1. Create a Maven project and add the JUnit 5 junit-jupiter dependency to pom.xml.
2. Create the VoteChecker class with the isEligible(int age) method.
3. Return true for age 18 or more and throw IllegalArgumentException for age below 0 or above 120.
4. Identify boundary values: -1, 0, 17, 18, 19, 120 and 121.
5. Write a @ParameterizedTest with @CsvSource for valid boundary values and verify using assertEquals().
6. Write another @ParameterizedTest with @ValueSource for invalid ages -1 and 121 using assertThrows().
7. Run tests using mvn test and verify the output.
CODE
VoteChecker.java
public class VoteChecker {
    public static boolean isEligible(int age) {
        if (age < 0 || age > 120) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid age");
        }
        return age >= 18;
    }
}


VoteCheckerTest.java
import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.CsvSource;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.ValueSource;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class VoteCheckerTest {

    @ParameterizedTest(name = "age {0} -> eligible {1}")
    @CsvSource({
        "0,false",
        "17,false",
        "18,true",
        "19,true",
        "120,true"
    })
    void validAges(int age, boolean expected) {
        assertEquals(expected, VoteChecker.isEligible(age));
    }

    @ParameterizedTest(name = "invalid age {0}")
    @ValueSource(ints = {-1, 121})
    void invalidAges(int age) {
        assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class,
            () -> VoteChecker.isEligible(age));
    }
}


